id: pm-pcys9n
title: "Release attribution is derived from a mutable timestamp while the immutable release stamp ships unused, so the changelog records what was touched instead of what shipped"
description: "The changelog generator resolves an item into a release window with closed_at ?? updated_at ?? created_at (pm-changelog generator.ts itemTimestamp). In this workspace 1,488 of 1,793 terminal items carry no closed_at, so 83 percent of shipped history is attributed by last-touch time. Any metadata write on those items — the exact writes the terminal relationship backfill, the close-reason backfill, and the duplicate-code rename all require — silently re-buckets long-shipped work into the current release. The generator already supports a timestamp-independent alternative: a release window can declare a releaseTag and items whose release field matches are bucketed there regardless of their timestamps. That field is plumbed end to end — create --release, update --release, list --release, list --filter-release-missing — and it is populated on 469 items, all of them terminal, with fourteen distinct values across which exactly one, v2026.3.12, matches one of the 76 real git tags. The rest are either a legacy taxonomy that predates date-based versioning (archive-2026q2 at 282 items, vnext at 144, v0.1 and variants at 34) or genuine release dates written without the v prefix the tags use (2026.5.14, 2026.6.10 and six more). Every active item carries no value at all. So the stable attribution key is not merely unused: it is populated with values that guarantee a miss, which reads as adopted while behaving as absent."
type: Issue
status: open
priority: 1
tags[6]: changelog,governance,history,provenance,release,tracker-governance
created_at: "2026-07-26T10:45:35.500Z"
updated_at: "2026-07-26T17:21:50.081Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 300
acceptance_criteria: "The release cut stamps the release identifier onto every item it publishes, as an ordinary history-recorded mutation, so attribution is written once at the moment it becomes true; The stamped identifier uses exactly the form the release windows are keyed by, and a check rejects a value that cannot match any tag rather than storing it silently; Existing values are normalized where they are recoverable — unprefixed release dates map to their tags — and the legacy taxonomy values are either mapped to a real release or recorded as deliberately unattributed, never left in a state that reads as adopted; Historical release membership is backfilled deterministically from the tag containing each item's closing history entry, with an explicit residual list for items whose evidence is insufficient rather than a guessed value; Changelog generation prefers the stamped release identifier over any timestamp, and the check gate proves that editing metadata on an already-published item produces no changelog diff; A validation surface reports items that are terminal and unattributed, so the gap is measurable instead of invisible; The published changelog is byte-identical across the migration for every already-released window"
goal: project management = context management
objective: Make release membership an immutable recorded fact rather than a side effect of the last edit
value: "Every governance backfill this tracker still owes — terminal relationship lineage, close-reason evidence, duplicate-code repair — is gated not by its own risk but by the changelog re-bucketing the items it touches. Attribution by a stable key removes that coupling entirely."
impact: "Unblocks the terminal-history graph program and the resolution-evidence backfills, and makes release archaeology answerable from the tracker instead of from git tags"
outcome: "Release membership survives arbitrary later edits, and the changelog states what shipped in each release rather than what was most recently modified"
why_now: "The terminal graph backfill is the largest remaining structural deficit in the tracker, and this is the single constraint that makes it expensive"
parent: pm-33cw
risk: medium
confidence: 80
