comments[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-26T10:58:03.069Z","harness:claude-code","Measurement correction, 2026-07-26. An earlier reading of this workspace reported zero items carrying a release value. That was wrong, and the cause is worth recording because it will mislead the next measurement too: pm list --json omits non-projected fields, so release never appears in list output and a jq filter over it returns zero regardless of the stored data. Reading the .toon files directly gives 469 items with a release value, all of them terminal, across fourteen distinct values. Exactly one of those fourteen (v2026.3.12) matches one of the 76 git tags in this repository. Nine are release dates written without the v prefix the tags carry, and four are a legacy taxonomy (archive-2026q2, vnext, v0.1, v1-archive) that predates date-based versioning. No active item carries a value. The corrected picture strengthens the case rather than weakening it: the field is populated enough to look adopted and mismatched enough that releaseTag bucketing cannot use it, so a migration has to normalize existing values as well as stamp new ones."
  "2026-07-26T17:21:50.080Z","harness:claude-code","Acceptance criterion four is delivered, and the mechanism question this item raised is settled — with a correction to the assumption behind it.\n\nThe item states that the generator supports a timestamp-independent alternative via a release window declaring a releaseTag. It is stronger than that: under --all-release-tags, which is the mode this repository's changelog scripts already use, the item release field is unconditionally the attribution authority. The help text says so directly. No generator change and no script change were required. This was proved before any bulk write: stamping release v2026.6.30 onto closed pm-j1fj, a write that moved its updated_at to the present, produced a byte-identical CHANGELOG.md at 296,342 bytes.\n\nBackfill executed and verified. Close instants derived from immutable history for all 1,795 terminal items: 1,683 from an op:close entry, 90 from an update that moved status into a terminal value, 22 from a create that was already terminal, zero fallbacks, zero unresolved. Control before writing: of the 307 items already carrying closed_at, the derived instant matched 307 and disagreed on 0. Mapping is the earliest tag whose creatordate is at or after the close instant, over 76 real tags. 1,782 stamped; the 13 that close after v2026.7.26 left unstamped, and they are exactly the current Unreleased membership. Post-write verification: 0 of 1,795 items disagree with the derivation.\n\nChangelog consequence, reviewed: regeneration moved 295 items, every move matched the derivation, no items were added or removed, Unreleased stayed at 12, and changelog:pm:check returns check true. The 295 moves are corrections — those items were previously bucketed by a bulk updated_at touch rather than by when they shipped. Acceptance criterion seven asks for byte-identical output across the migration; that is the correct requirement for a mechanism change but not for a data correction, and the honest result is recorded here rather than claimed.\n\nRemaining on this item: stamping at release-cut time so new closures are attributed automatically, the validation surface that reports terminal-and-unattributed items, the rejection of values that cannot match a tag, and the check gate proving a metadata write on a published item produces no diff. Without the first of those, attribution decays one release at a time. Delivery evidence lives on pm-3j6it6.\n"
notes[2]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-26T10:58:16.506Z","harness:claude-code","Related read-surface trap worth fixing alongside this: pm list --json does not project the release field, so list --release filters correctly while the field itself is invisible in the output. Any migration script or audit written against list --json will measure zero and conclude the field is unused. pm list --filter-release-missing does work and reports all 275 active items as missing. The projection gap belongs to the list-projection family rather than to this item, but the migration must not be measured through it."
  "2026-07-26T11:02:09.522Z","harness:claude-code","The mechanism is already shipped and merely unused: pm changelog generate accepts --respect-item-release, documented as treating an item release field as the authority. The generation script for this repository does not pass it, and could not usefully pass it today because only one of the fourteen stored values matches a tag. That makes the delivery order concrete — normalize and stamp the values first, then enable the flag in changelog:pm and changelog:pm:check, then prove the drag is gone by editing metadata on a published item and observing an unchanged changelog. No new generator capability is required."
body: ""
